== Motivation ==

The BLAKE2b proof-of-work specification adds fields to the block header, but defines only their widths and meanings, not how they are serialized.
Peers need a common serialization to exchange version 2 headers and blocks.
Software that understands only the historical 80-byte header cannot parse them, so peers also need a way to tell which peers can.

== Specification ==

=== Block-header serialization ===

When version bit 31 is clear, the historical 80-byte header is used.
When it is set, the serialized block header is 164 bytes:
the historical 80-byte header followed by an 84-byte extension.
This version-selected length applies wherever a block header is serialized in a P2P message, including <code>block</code>, <code>headers</code>, <code>cmpctblock</code>, and <code>merkleblock</code> messages.

The historical header's fields are serialized as before:

{| class="wikitable"
! Byte offset
! Bit length
! Field
! Serialization notes
|-
| 0 || 32 || Version
| Bit 31 is set
|-
| 4 || 256 || Previous block
|
|-
| 36 || 256 || Merkle root
|
|-
| 68 || 32 || Timestamp
|
|-
| 72 || 32 || Target (<code>nBits</code>)
|
|-
| 76 || 32 || Nonce
| First 32 bits; the historical nonce field
|}

The extension consists of the following fields.
Integers are unsigned and little-endian;
other fields are byte strings.
Bits within the byte at offset 110 are numbered from least significant (0) to most significant (7).

{| class="wikitable"
! Byte offset
! Bit length
! Field
! Serialization notes
|-
| 80 || 64 || Nonce
| Last 64 bits
|-
| 88 || 128 || Extranonce
|
|-
| 104 || 32 || Time offset
|
|-
| 108 || 16 || Transaction count
|
|-
| rowspan="4" | 110
| 2 || Hashing-hardware profile selector
| Bits 0-1
|-
| 1 || Time-offset enable bit
| Bit 2
|-
| 3 || Reserved bits
| Bits 3-5
|-
| 2 || Padding
| Bits 6-7; MUST be zero
|-
| 111 || 8 || XOR-mask clear-bit count
|
|-
| 112 || 128 || XOR key
|
|-
| 128 || 32 || Height
| The 31-bit height; the most significant bit is padding and MUST be zero
|-
| 132 || 256 || Merge-mining right-hand side
|
|}

A header with nonzero padding is malformed.
This is a parse error rather than a consensus rule, but implementations MAY treat it as they would an invalid block.

=== Data following the header ===

In a <code>headers</code> message, each header MUST still be followed by a terminating zero byte.
In other messages, a transaction count accompanying a version 2 header, such as the CompactSize prefix of a block body or the 32-bit count in a <code>merkleblock</code> message, MUST equal the transaction count committed in the header.
A message violating these requirements is malformed, but because its header may still be valid, implementations MUST NOT cache that header as invalid.

=== Service bit ===

This document defines the following service bit:

<pre>
NODE_BLAKE2B = 1 << 28
</pre>

<code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> indicates that the node supports the version 2 block-header serialization.
All peer-to-peer software supporting it MUST set this bit in the services field of its <code>version</code> message, including before activation, whether or not it validates blocks.
Software that validates blocks SHOULD also include it in any announcement of its own address, such as in <code>addr</code> or <code>addrv2</code> messages; other software MAY do so.
For example, a chain indexer needs to set the bit in its <code>version</code> message in order to be served version 2 headers and blocks.

The bit is an unauthenticated advertisement.
Implementations MUST remain robust if a peer advertises it incorrectly.

<code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> implies support for witness serialization:
a node advertising it MUST serve witness data as if it also advertised <code>NODE_WITNESS</code>.
This upgrade therefore deprecates <code>NODE_WITNESS</code>.
Software that would require <code>NODE_WITNESS</code> of a peer SHOULD accept <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> in its place.
Nodes MAY continue to advertise <code>NODE_WITNESS</code>.

This upgrade also deprecates the temporary <code>NODE_REDUCED_DATA</code> service bit (<code>1 << 27</code>).
Software implementing this specification need neither advertise it nor act on it.

=== Peer selection ===

This section is advisory and not normative.

Software will generally prefer outbound peers that advertise <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code>.
As with <code>NODE_WITNESS</code>, the bit indicates only serialization support, but serves as a practical heuristic for peers that follow the valid chain and can provide post-activation blocks.
The reference implementation keeps a bounded number of otherwise useful outbound peers that lack the bit as additional connections, without letting them displace its outbound target.
When deciding whether its address manager provides enough bootstrap coverage, it counts only addresses advertising <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code>, rather than treating an address manager populated only by obsolete peers as sufficient.
Software querying address sources, such as DNS seeds, with service-bit filters will generally want to request <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> without <code>NODE_WITNESS</code>, so as not to exclude upgraded nodes that no longer advertise <code>NODE_WITNESS</code>.

=== Header synchronization and block relay ===

Nodes SHOULD NOT serve or announce version 2 blocks or headers to peers that do not advertise <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code>.
Nodes MAY also refuse to serve or announce any headers or blocks to such peers.
When the upgrade is scheduled or active, a node SHOULD NOT send such peers a <code>getheaders</code> message unless its <code>hash_stop</code> identifies the final SHA256d block or an ancestor thereof.
On mainnet, block 961,631 (<code>00000000000000000000807f9dc917442a67910426d79ebb2f8aa2149327ce8a</code>) takes the place of the final SHA256d block in this rule.

== Rationale ==

'''What is the purpose of the reserved bits and the bits that must be zero?'''

Both allow a future protocol upgrade to assign meanings to them.
Nodes implementing only the current rules ignore the reserved bits in the byte at offset 110, so a future upgrade can give them meanings without invalidating blocks for those nodes.
Headers with any of the bits that must be zero set (bits 6-7 of the byte at offset 110 and the most significant bit of the height) remain explicitly malformed to such nodes, so these bits suit backward-incompatible upgrades similar to the BLAKE2b upgrade itself.
For example, the most significant bit of the height could be used to extend it when the 31-bit range runs out around AD 42,838.

'''Why a new service bit?'''

Nodes that know only the historical 80-byte header format cannot parse the version 2 serialization.
The service bit tells a node which peers can, so that it sends version 2 headers and blocks only to those peers, without needlessly disconnecting older nodes, which may still be useful for transaction relay, peer relay and historical blocks.
It also helps upgraded nodes find peers able to serve them post-activation headers and blocks.
Like all service bits, it is only a claim, which normal block validation must still verify.

'''Why does NODE_BLAKE2B use a temporary-range service bit?'''

Service bits 24 to 31 are reserved for temporary experiments and other temporary uses.
<code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> was originally intended merely to help upgraded nodes find each other during the upgrade, which is such a temporary use.
Only later was it extended to negotiate the version 2 serialization, which nodes rely on indefinitely.

'''Why does NODE_BLAKE2B supersede NODE_WITNESS?'''

All software supporting the version 2 serialization also supports witness serialization, so a separate bit adds no information.
<code>NODE_WITNESS</code> remains meaningful only for peers without <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code>, such as obsolete nodes used for initial block download.
Not requiring it from upgraded peers allows the bit to eventually be freed for other uses.

'''Why not keep NODE_WITNESS for nodes that discard historical witness data?'''

If <code>NODE_WITNESS</code> remained meaningful, such a node could have served historical blocks without their witnesses, signaling this by not advertising <code>NODE_WITNESS</code>.
Since <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> requires serving witness data, this is not possible under this upgrade.
This is no loss: blocks without witnesses are useful only to software that does not fully validate them, which is not something to encourage.
For a fully validating node, a node lacking historical witness data is simply pruned, and advertises <code>NODE_NETWORK_LIMITED</code> or no block-serving service bit accordingly.

'''Why may nodes continue to advertise NODE_WITNESS?'''

Software that does not yet accept <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> in its place, including obsolete software, may still select peers by it, so advertising it keeps a node selectable by such software.
As software adopts this upgrade, this matters less and less.

'''Why deprecate NODE_REDUCED_DATA?'''

Some nodes used <code>NODE_REDUCED_DATA</code>, a temporary service bit that was not defined by a BIP, to indicate enforcement of BIP 110's rules.
Validating nodes on the upgraded chain already enforce those rules where applicable, so a separate signal is no longer needed.
<code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> does not itself indicate whether a peer validates blocks.

'''Why limit what is served to peers without NODE_BLAKE2B?'''

A peer without <code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> cannot parse version 2 headers or blocks.
Having not upgraded, it may also follow an invalid altcoin chain, which diverged from mainnet at height 961,632.
Nodes have no obligation to serve such peers anything.
Such a peer may nonetheless be willing to serve historical Bitcoin blocks, so a node may still use it for initial block download.
If a node requests headers from such a peer, it should use a <code>hash_stop</code> identifying the block specified above or an ancestor thereof to limit the response.
If the response reaches that block, the peer should stop there, and the response should not include headers from the altcoin chain.

== Backward compatibility ==

Peers that understand only the historical 80-byte header cannot parse the version 2 form.
<code>NODE_BLAKE2B</code> helps implementations select compatible peers, but it is an unauthenticated advertisement and does not guarantee a peer's behavior.

